<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Fast-scroller thumb (see FastScrollerHelper): a circle with its right side cut off flat so it sits
    against the screen edge, with stacked up/down triangles centred inside.

    GEOMETRY - derived, not eyeballed. Full circle r=26 centred at (26,26), so it spans x 0..52; the flat
    right edge cuts it at x=40, keeping 40/52 = 77% of the width ("roughly three-quarters of a circle").
    The chord at x=40 is at dx=14 from the centre, so its half-height is sqrt(26^2 - 14^2) = sqrt(480) =
    21.909 - hence the arc endpoints at y = 26 +/- 21.909 = 4.091 and 47.909. The arc is large-arc-flag=1
    (it wraps the left side, well over half the circle) and sweep-flag=0 (counterclockwise on screen from
    top-right, through the leftmost point, to bottom-right). `Z` closes it with the flat right edge.
    ⚠️ If you change the width, recompute the endpoints - they are not free parameters. Changing
    viewportWidth alone moves the cut without moving the arc, and the shape stops closing cleanly.

    The drawable is ONLY the visible shape: viewport == the visible bounds, no overhang and no negative
    padding, so it lays out at the edge exactly like the shape it replaced. Both intrinsic dimensions are
    declared, which the FastScroller constructor requires (it range-checks the thumb's intrinsic width AND
    height; the track is checked on width only).

    NOT FLUSH TO THE PHYSICAL EDGE, by inheritance: applyTo sets 8dp padding on all four sides, and
    MainFragment.applyInsets re-pads with insets + 8dp, so the flat edge stops 8dp short. That is
    unchanged from the previous thumb. Zero the RIGHT padding in both places if true flush is wanted -
    it is a value change, not a geometry change here.

    TRIANGLES centred at x=21: that is the optical centre, not the arithmetic one. The bounding box's
    middle is x=20 and the shape's mass centre is near x=24 (the cut only removes a thin right cap of a
    circle centred at 26), and 21 balances the 16dp of curve on the left against 14dp to the flat edge.
    10dp wide, 6dp tall, 6dp apart, so the pair spans y 17..35 inside a 52dp body.

    COLOURS are semantic tokens, not literals - the theme is Theme.Material3Expressive.DynamicColors.
    DayNight, so a hardcoded white body would be right in light and wrong in dark.
    colorSurfaceContainerHighest reads near-white in light and stays distinct from the page background in
    dark, which is what "white fill" means here in a DayNight app. Theme attrs resolve in a vector's
    fillColor from API 21 when inflated with a theme; minSdk is 24 and FastScrollerHelper loads this
    through AppCompatResources with an Activity context.

    Triangles use colorOnSurfaceVariant - their OWN token, deliberately NOT a translucent surface colour.
    The body is opaque, so a translucent triangle composites against the body rather than the content,
    which means its apparent grey shifts every time the body token changes and has to be re-tuned per
    theme. colorOnSurfaceVariant IS the M3 token for muted content on a surface and is guaranteed legible
    against colorSurface* in both modes; alpha would lose contrast in dark, where surface-on-surface has
    much less headroom to work with.

    The 1dp outline is what carries the shape over busy cover art - the same reason the previous thumb
    grew one. At this size it is a refinement rather than the load-bearing element it was at 12dp.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="40dp"
    android:height="52dp"
    android:viewportWidth="40"
    android:viewportHeight="52">

    <!-- Body: chopped circle, flat edge on the right. -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="?attr/colorSurfaceContainerHighest"
        android:pathData="M40,4.091 A26,26 0 1 0 40,47.909 Z"
        android:strokeColor="?attr/colorOutlineVariant"
        android:strokeWidth="1" />

    <!-- Up triangle. -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="?attr/colorOnSurfaceVariant"
        android:pathData="M21,17 L26,23 L16,23 Z" />

    <!-- Down triangle. -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="?attr/colorOnSurfaceVariant"
        android:pathData="M21,35 L26,29 L16,29 Z" />

</vector>
